---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../consts';
---

<Page
	title="Editorial Policy"
	description="The standards every ProofNews fact-check is published under: how we choose claims, rank sources, decide verdicts, and review each article before it goes live."
	eyebrow="Our Standards"
>
	<p>
		This page sets out the standards every ProofNews fact-check is published under. It is the
		same checklist we work to internally, published so readers can hold us to it. If you
		believe an article falls short of these standards, <a href="/contact">contact us</a> — and
		if it contains an error, our <a href="/corrections">Corrections</a> page explains what
		happens next.
	</p>

	<h2>1. Claim Selection</h2>
	<p>
		We check claims, not people. A claim qualifies for a fact-check when it meets three tests:
		it is <strong>widely shared</strong> (circulating on social media, in forwarded messages,
		or in public discussion), it is <strong>consequential</strong> (a reader who believed it
		might change a health decision, a purchase, a vote, or a belief about how the world works),
		and it is <strong>checkable</strong> (it asserts something factual that evidence can
		support or refute). Opinions, predictions, and satire are not fact-checked as if they were
		factual claims; when we cover them, the article says so.
	</p>

	<h2>2. Sourcing Standards</h2>
	<p>Sources are ranked, and every fact-check is built from the highest rank available:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Primary research</strong> — peer-reviewed studies, systematic reviews, and
			meta-analyses, read in the original where possible, not just as reported elsewhere.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Official sources</strong> — guidance, data, and statements from the institutions
			responsible for the subject, such as the WHO, CDC, FDA, NASA, and national statistics
			agencies.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Reputable reporting</strong> — established news organisations and specialist
			publications, used for context and for claims they directly witnessed or documented.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Never sufficient on their own</strong> — social media posts, anonymous claims,
			screenshot chains, and content farms. These are how claims reach us, never how we
			verify them.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Every fact-check ends with a <strong>Sources</strong> section linking the primary material
		used, so readers can inspect the same evidence we did. A single small study does not
		settle a question that a body of research answers differently, and our articles say which
		situation applies.
	</p>

	<h2>3. How Verdicts Are Decided</h2>
	<p>
		Each fact-check ends in one of five verdicts — <strong>True</strong>,
		<strong>Mostly True</strong>, <strong>Misleading</strong>, <strong>False</strong>, or
		<strong>Unproven</strong>. The verdict is assigned after the research, not before it, and
		it follows the weight of the evidence: the rank and agreement of the sources, the size and
		quality of the studies behind them, and how directly they address the claim as stated.
		When the evidence is genuinely divided or absent, the verdict is Unproven rather than a
		guess in either direction. Verdicts are also recorded in each article's structured data
		(ClaimReview), so the rating is machine-readable and cannot quietly differ from the text.
	</p>

	<h2>4. Review Before Publication</h2>
	<p>
		No fact-check is published straight from a first draft. Before publication, every article
		is reviewed against its own sources: each figure, date, name, and quotation is checked
		back against the material it came from; quotations are kept short and attributed; and the
		verdict is re-tested against the evidence one final time. If a claim in the draft cannot
		be traced to a real, checkable source, it is removed or the article is not published.
	</p>

	<h2>5. How Our Content Is Made</h2>
	<p>
		ProofNews researches and drafts its articles with the help of modern research tools, under
		the sourcing and review standards on this page. Tools can gather and organise material,
		but they do not decide verdicts and they are never cited as sources: every fact in a
		published article must trace to a real source a reader can open. We do not publish
		invented experts, fabricated quotations, or synthetic eyewitnesses, and our articles are
		published under the ProofNews name as an organisation rather than under invented
		individual bylines.
	</p>

	<h2>6. Independence and Conflicts</h2>
	<p>
		ProofNews is independently published and advertising supported. Advertising has no
		influence over which claims we select or which verdicts we reach, and no subject of a
		fact-check is given advance sight of a verdict in exchange for anything. Where an article
		discusses a product or service, the verdict rests on the same evidence standard as any
		other claim.
	</p>

	<h2>7. Corrections and Updates</h2>
	<p>
		Errors are corrected promptly and openly under our
		<a href="/corrections">Corrections</a> policy: significant corrections are noted on the
		article itself with a date, and a verdict that changes is explained, not silently swapped.
		If the evidence behind a published verdict is overturned by new research, we update the
		article and say why. To report an error or suggest a claim, email us at
		<a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
	</p>
</Page>
